---
import ContentPage from '../components/ContentPage.astro';
import type { FaqItem } from '../lib/jsonld';

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
	{
		question: 'Do you store or upload my JSON?',
		answer:
			'No. Every JSON document you paste, type, or open is processed entirely in your browser using client-side JavaScript. It is never uploaded, transmitted, logged, or stored on any server. You can verify this in your browser dev-tools network tab, or by using the tools fully offline.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Do you use cookies?',
		answer:
			'Yes — but not for your data. Like most free, ad-supported sites, JSON Beam uses cookies for privacy-respecting analytics (Google Analytics) and advertising (Google AdSense), and stores a small preference (your theme) locally. None of this ever includes the JSON you process, which stays in your browser. You can control or refuse the analytics and advertising cookies — see our cookie policy.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Can I use JSON Beam offline?',
		answer:
			'Yes. Because all JSON processing happens client-side, the tools keep working with no network connection once the page has loaded. Working offline is the strongest guarantee that the data you process stays with you.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Do you run ads or analytics?',
		answer:
			'Yes. We run Google Analytics to understand aggregate traffic and Google AdSense to show ads, which keeps the tools free. Both are standard third-party Google services that see page-level activity only — never the content of the JSON you work with, which is never transmitted, sold, or shared.',
	},
];
---

<ContentPage
	slug="/privacy"
	title="Privacy Policy — JSON Beam"
	description="JSON Beam's privacy policy: the JSON you process stays in your browser and is never uploaded. Read how we use cookies, Google Analytics, and AdSense."
	breadcrumbLabel="Privacy Policy"
	h1="Privacy policy."
	intro="Two things are true at once: the JSON you process never leaves your browser, and — like most free sites — we use cookies for analytics and ads. This page explains exactly what each of those means."
	lastUpdated="July 8, 2026"
	faqs={faqs}
>
	<h2>The short version</h2>
	<p>
		JSON Beam is a <strong>100% client-side</strong> JSON workbench. The JSON you paste, type,
		or open is processed entirely inside your own browser and is
		<strong>never uploaded, logged, transmitted, sold, or shared</strong>. There is no server
		that receives your documents, and no account is required.
	</p>
	<p>
		Separately, the website itself — the pages and static files — uses cookies and similar
		technologies for <strong>privacy-respecting analytics</strong> (Google Analytics) and
		<strong>advertising</strong> (Google AdSense), which is how we keep the tools free. These
		operate at the level of ordinary page visits and <strong>never touch the content of the
		JSON you work with</strong>. This page explains both sides in plain language, and our
		<a href="/cookie-policy">cookie policy</a> lists the specific cookies and how to control them.
	</p>

	<h2>1. The data you process stays on your device</h2>
	<p>
		Formatting, validating, repairing, querying, comparing, visualizing, and converting all
		happen locally, on your machine, using JavaScript that runs in your browser. When you
		<a href="/json-formatter">format JSON</a>, <a href="/json-validator">validate it</a>, or
		<a href="/json-diff">compare two documents</a>, that content is never sent anywhere. There
		is no upload step, no API call carrying your payload, and no copy kept on our side —
		because there is no “our side” in the loop. This is why you can safely work with API keys,
		customer records, access tokens, and proprietary configuration files.
	</p>

	<h2>2. Information stored locally in your browser</h2>
	<p>
		To remember your preferences between visits, we store a small amount of data in your
		browser’s <code>localStorage</code> — for example, whether you prefer the light or dark
		editor theme (the <code>jb-theme</code> key). This data:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>lives only in your browser on your device;</li>
		<li>is never transmitted to us or any third party;</li>
		<li>can be cleared at any time by clearing your site data in your browser.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>3. Information collected automatically</h2>
	<p>
		Like virtually every website, the static files that make up JSON Beam are delivered by a
		hosting and content-delivery provider. When your browser requests those files, the provider
		may automatically process standard technical request metadata — such as your IP address,
		browser type, and the time of the request — to deliver the page, keep the service secure,
		and prevent abuse. This is ordinary web-server information; it is not linked to the JSON you
		process, and it never includes the content of your documents.
	</p>

	<h2>4. Analytics (Google Analytics)</h2>
	<p>
		We use <strong>Google Analytics</strong>, a web-analytics service provided by Google, to
		understand — in aggregate — how many people visit, which pages and tools are used, and how
		the site performs. Google Analytics sets cookies (such as <code>_ga</code> and
		<code>_ga_&lt;id&gt;</code>) and processes information like your device, approximate
		location, and the pages you view, so we can improve the tools.
	</p>
	<p>
		This measurement is about <strong>page-level activity, not your data</strong> — Google
		Analytics never receives the JSON you process. Google acts as a data processor for this
		usage information. You can learn how Google handles data at the
		<a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank"
			>Google Privacy Policy</a
		>
		and <a
			href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
			rel="noopener nofollow"
			target="_blank">How Google uses information from sites that use its services</a
		>, and you can opt out across all sites with the
		<a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank"
			>Google Analytics Opt-out Browser Add-on</a
		>.
	</p>

	<h2>5. Advertising (Google AdSense)</h2>
	<p>
		JSON Beam displays ads through <strong>Google AdSense</strong> to keep the tools free. In
		serving those ads:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Third-party vendors, <strong>including Google</strong>, use cookies to serve ads based on
			your prior visits to this and other websites.
		</li>
		<li>
			Google’s use of advertising cookies (including the <code>DoubleClick</code> /
			<code>__gads</code> and <code>__eoi</code> cookies) enables it and its partners to serve
			ads to you based on your visits.
		</li>
		<li>
			You can opt out of personalized advertising by visiting
			<a href="https://www.google.com/settings/ads" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank"
				>Google Ads Settings</a
			>. You can also opt out of some third-party vendors’ use of cookies at
			<a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank"
				>aboutads.info/choices</a
			>
			or, in the EU, <a
				href="https://www.youronlinechoices.eu/"
				rel="noopener nofollow"
				target="_blank">youronlinechoices.eu</a
			>.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Where you have not consented to personalized ads (for example, before granting consent in
		the EEA or UK — see the next section), ads may still be shown on a non-personalized basis.
		As with analytics, ad serving operates on page-level signals and <strong>never receives the
		JSON you process</strong>. For the full list of cookies, see the
		<a href="/cookie-policy">cookie policy</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>6. Your consent choices</h2>
	<p>
		For visitors in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, and Switzerland, we use
		Google’s certified consent management platform together with
		<strong>Google Consent Mode</strong>. Non-essential analytics and advertising cookies are
		<strong>withheld until you make a choice</strong>, and you can grant or refuse them, and
		change your decision later. Elsewhere, these cookies may be set by default, and you can still
		opt out using the links in the sections above and in our
		<a href="/cookie-policy">cookie policy</a>. You can also block or delete cookies at any time
		through your browser settings.
	</p>

	<h2>7. Third-party services</h2>
	<p>
		Beyond Google Analytics and AdSense (described above), the site relies on a hosting/CDN
		provider that serves the static files, as described in section 3. We self-host our fonts, so
		no external font network requests are made.
	</p>
	<p>
		If — and only if — you choose to send us a message through our
		<a href="/contact">contact form</a>, that message is delivered to us by email through
		Web3Forms, a third-party form-to-email provider. Web3Forms processes the details you enter
		(such as your name, email address, and message) solely to deliver them to us. We receive only
		what you type, and only when you press Send; we use it to reply to you, and we do not sell or
		share it. Using the form is entirely optional — it exists only so you can reach us without
		our having to publish an email address.
	</p>

	<h2>8. Data security</h2>
	<p>
		The most effective security measure is architectural: because the JSON you process never
		leaves your browser, there is no central store of user data to breach, leak, or subpoena. The
		site is served over HTTPS so the application files reach you intact and untampered.
	</p>

	<h2>9. Children’s privacy</h2>
	<p>
		JSON Beam is a general-purpose developer tool and is not directed at children. We do not
		knowingly collect personal information from anyone, including children under 13.
	</p>

	<h2>10. Your rights</h2>
	<p>
		We do not build user profiles or store the JSON you process, so there is no document archive
		to access, correct, export, or delete on our servers. For the usage data handled by Google
		Analytics and AdSense, your rights — including access, deletion, and objection under laws
		such as the GDPR and CCPA — can be exercised through Google’s controls linked above and
		through your own browser and Google account settings, or by contacting us. You remain in full
		control of the JSON you work with and of the preferences stored in your own browser.
	</p>

	<h2>11. Changes to this policy</h2>
	<p>
		We may update this policy as the product evolves — for instance, if we add new features or
		services. When we do, we will revise the “Last updated” date at the top of this page.
		Material changes will be made clear. Our core commitment will not change: the content you
		process stays private to you.
	</p>

	<h2>12. Contact us</h2>
	<p>
		If you have any questions about this privacy policy or how JSON Beam handles data, send us a
		message through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
	</p>
</ContentPage>
